//! The String module: byte-wise operations on a `string`, none of which allocate.
//!
//! Written in Jairs, like `Basic`, because PLAN.md decision #5 puts the standard library in the language
//! rather than in the compiler. This is the second module the project has, and the first that is not
//! `Basic` — so it is also the first real exercise of importing two modules at once (ADR-0014's flat merge).
//!
//! # Why this module exists, and why it is W7's first
//!
//! ADR-0099 §4 refused `==` on two strings: a `string` is `{data: *u8, count: s64}` (ADR-0004), so "the same
//! storage" and "the same contents" are both plausible readings, and picking one silently would make the
//! other a bug that looks like working code. That refusal's own reasoning named the fix — comparing contents
//! needs a byte loop — and a byte loop is a *library*'s job, not an operator's. So the previous wave named
//! this module as the answer to a refusal it raised, which is the clearest possible reason to write it first.
//!
//! An `==` that looped would also be the only implicitly-looping operator in the language, which is a much
//! larger thing to introduce than a procedure called `equal`.
//!
//! # Why nothing here allocates
//!
//! `concat`, `substring`, `to_upper_copy` and `split` are all deliberately absent. Each needs somewhere to put its
//! result, which means an allocator argument and a decision about who frees it. `context.allocator` exists
//! (ADR-0057) and so does temporary storage (ADR-0065), so the mechanism is not missing — but *choosing*
//! between "always the context allocator", "an explicit parameter" and "always temporary" has real
//! consequences for every caller, and it deserves its own decision rather than being settled in passing by
//! whichever one this file happened to use.
//!
//! A non-allocating module is a complete and useful thing on its own. Shipping it first means the allocation
//! decision gets made with a working baseline to compare against.
//!
//! # Why `byte_at` exists at all
//!
//! `s.data[i]` **does not compile**: `data` is a `*u8` and a pointer is not indexable (E0234). Reading a byte
//! therefore takes `(s.data + i).*` plus a cast to `s64`, which is pointer arithmetic in the middle of
//! otherwise ordinary code. `byte_at` is that expression with a name, and it is honest about being a
//! workaround: when pointer indexing arrives, this becomes a one-line wrapper rather than the only way.
//!
//! # What is checked, and where
//!
//! `tests/corpus/valid/084-string.jr` exercises every procedure here and exits on a value that depends on all
//! of them, so both engines must agree on each answer. That matters more than usual for this module: a
//! comparison routine that returned a constant would pass any test that only asked "did it run".
//!
//! # The allocating half, and where its memory comes from
//!
//! `concat`, `substring`, `to_upper_copy` and `to_lower_copy` each produce a **new** string, so each allocates — through
//! `context.allocator` (ADR-0062), and the **caller frees** with `free_string`. That is the convention ADR-0103
//! §3 deferred, chosen in ADR-0111: not `talloc`, because a result that silently expires on an unrelated
//! `reset_temporary_storage()` is a trap; not an explicit allocator parameter, because the context exists to
//! carry exactly this (ADR-0001) and a caller who wants arena behaviour installs an arena and gets it for every
//! routine at once.
//!
//! **A caller must install an allocator first.** `context.allocator` is null until then (ADR-0057 §5), and
//! calling a null one traps (ADR-0110) — so a program that concatenates without installing an allocator gets a
//! trap naming the null pointer, not a silent wrong answer. A failed allocation returns `""`, because ADR-0058
//! §4's line is that a trap is for a *program* error and running out of memory is not one.
//!
//! **And it needs no `#import "Basic"`.** The allocator comes from `context`, which is a *language* facility
//! (ADR-0057) rather than a library name — so this module reaches its allocation through a hidden parameter every
//! procedure already has, and imports nothing at all. A `#import "Basic"` here was an unused import (E0231), and
//! the warning was right.

/// The byte at `index`, or `-1` when the index is out of range.
///
/// **`-1` rather than a trap**, unlike an out-of-range array index (ADR-0003): an array's bound is known to
/// the compiler and indexing past it is a *mistake*, while scanning a string until the bytes run out is an
/// ordinary way to write a loop. A caller who wants the strict reading can compare against `count` first.
///
/// The cast to `s64` is not a convenience: a `u8` cannot be compared to an integer literal without one
/// (ADR-0016 §1 gives a literal its type from context, and there is no implicit widening — ADR-0015), so
/// returning `u8` would push a cast into every caller.
byte_at :: (s: string, index: s64) -> s64 {
    if index < 0 {
        return -1;
    }
    if index >= s.count {
        return -1;
    }
    p := s.data + index;
    return cast(s64, p.*);
}

/// Whether `s` has no bytes.
///
/// One line, and worth a name: `is_empty(s)` says what it means at a call site where `s.count == 0` says how
/// it is measured.
is_empty :: (s: string) -> bool {
    return s.count == 0;
}

/// Whether `a` and `b` have the same bytes.
///
/// **The reason this module exists.** E0278's help says "compare `.count`, or compare fields one at a time";
/// this is the real answer it was pointing at.
///
/// Lengths are compared first, which is not merely an optimisation: it is what makes the loop below safe to
/// write against one bound instead of two.
equal :: (a: string, b: string) -> bool {
    if a.count != b.count {
        return false;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < a.count {
        if byte_at(a, i) != byte_at(b, i) {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Negative when `a` sorts before `b`, zero when they are equal, positive when after.
///
/// Byte order, not locale or case: a `string` is bytes (ADR-0004), and anything else would need a notion of
/// encoding this language does not have. A shorter string that is a prefix of a longer one sorts first, which
/// is the ordinary lexicographic rule.
///
/// The **sign** is what is specified, not the magnitude — a caller must not read the difference of two bytes
/// out of it, because that would pin an implementation detail no other comparison routine promises.
compare :: (a: string, b: string) -> s64 {
    i := 0;
    while i < a.count {
        if i >= b.count {
            // `a` is longer and matched so far, so `b` is a prefix of `a`.
            return 1;
        }
        x := byte_at(a, i);
        y := byte_at(b, i);
        if x < y {
            return -1;
        }
        if x > y {
            return 1;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if b.count > a.count {
        return -1;
    }
    return 0;
}

/// Whether `s` begins with `prefix`.
///
/// An empty prefix is `true`, which is the reading every other language takes and the one that makes
/// `starts_with(s, "")` composable rather than a special case a caller has to guard.
starts_with :: (s: string, prefix: string) -> bool {
    if prefix.count > s.count {
        return false;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < prefix.count {
        if byte_at(s, i) != byte_at(prefix, i) {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Whether `s` ends with `suffix`.
///
/// The offset is computed once rather than being folded into the index expression, because the interesting
/// thing about this procedure is that it reads `s` from a *different* starting point than `starts_with`, and
/// naming the offset is what makes that visible.
ends_with :: (s: string, suffix: string) -> bool {
    if suffix.count > s.count {
        return false;
    }
    offset := s.count - suffix.count;
    i := 0;
    while i < suffix.count {
        if byte_at(s, offset + i) != byte_at(suffix, i) {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// The index of the first occurrence of `needle` in `haystack`, or `-1`.
///
/// **`-1` rather than a second return value**, even though Jairs has multiple returns (ADR-0008): a caller of
/// `find` almost always feeds the result straight into a comparison, and `if find(h, n) >= 0` reads better than
/// naming an index it is about to discard. The error model earns its keep where the *value* is meaningless on
/// failure; here the sentinel is outside the domain of valid indices, so it cannot be mistaken for one.
///
/// An empty needle is found at 0, which follows from "every string starts with the empty string".
find :: (haystack: string, needle: string) -> s64 {
    if needle.count > haystack.count {
        return -1;
    }
    // The last start position worth trying: past it the needle cannot fit.
    last := haystack.count - needle.count;
    start := 0;
    while start <= last {
        i := 0;
        matched := true;
        while i < needle.count {
            if byte_at(haystack, start + i) != byte_at(needle, i) {
                matched = false;
                // The inner loop stops on the first mismatch rather than running to the end: `while` has no
                // `break` out of a nested loop without a label (ADR-0049), and setting the bound is clearer
                // here than labelling.
                i = needle.count;
            } else {
                i = i + 1;
            }
        }
        if matched {
            return start;
        }
        start = start + 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

/// Whether `needle` occurs anywhere in `haystack`.
///
/// Named separately from `find` because a predicate at a call site should not have to mention an index it does
/// not use — the same argument `is_empty` makes against `count == 0`.
contains :: (haystack: string, needle: string) -> bool {
    return find(haystack, needle) >= 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The allocating half (ADR-0111): each returns a new string the caller frees.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Two strings joined into a new one, allocated through `context.allocator`.
///
/// The caller frees the result with `free_string`. Concatenating two empty strings gives `""`, which needs no
/// allocation and none is made — so `free_string` on it is a no-op, which is the honest answer rather than a
/// zero-byte allocation to track.
///
/// A failed allocation returns `""`. That is distinguishable from every non-empty result, and a caller joining
/// two non-empty strings and getting `""` back has detected the failure.
concat :: (a: string, b: string) -> string {
    total := a.count + b.count;
    if total == 0 {
        return make_string(null, 0);
    }
    buf := context.allocator(total);
    if buf == null {
        return make_string(null, 0);
    }
    copy_bytes(buf, a.data, 0, a.count);
    copy_bytes(buf, b.data, a.count, b.count);
    return make_string(buf, total);
}

/// The `count` bytes of `s` starting at `start`, as a new string the caller frees.
///
/// An out-of-range request is **clamped** rather than trapping: asking for more than remains gives what remains,
/// and a `start` past the end gives `""`. Clamping rather than trapping because a substring of "whatever is
/// there, up to n" is an ordinary thing to want at the end of a string, the same reasoning `byte_at`'s `-1`
/// follows.
substring :: (s: string, start: s64, count: s64) -> string {
    from := start;
    if from < 0 {
        from = 0;
    }
    if from > s.count {
        from = s.count;
    }
    take := count;
    if take < 0 {
        take = 0;
    }
    if from + take > s.count {
        take = s.count - from;
    }
    if take == 0 {
        return make_string(null, 0);
    }
    buf := context.allocator(take);
    if buf == null {
        return make_string(null, 0);
    }
    copy_bytes(buf, s.data + from, 0, take);
    return make_string(buf, take);
}

/// A copy of `s` with ASCII lowercase letters raised to uppercase; other bytes unchanged.
///
/// ASCII only, and said so: a `string` is bytes (ADR-0004), and case-folding beyond ASCII needs a Unicode table
/// this language does not have. `'a'` is 97 and `'z'` is 122; subtracting 32 reaches `'A'`.
///
/// # Why the `_copy` suffix
///
/// **Renamed from `to_upper` by ADR-0197 §7**, and forced rather than chosen: `Basic` gained a `to_upper`
/// taking a **`u8`** — where Jai puts byte classification and where a lexer wants it — and `#import` is
/// flat, so a file importing both modules unqualified got E0211 on every use. E0211 firing is the good
/// outcome; the fix is that a module must prefix as though the namespace were its own (AGENTS.md).
///
/// `to_upper_copy` is **Jai's own name** for exactly this pair, so the collision resolved into the naming
/// the language being followed already had. The suffix also earns its keep: it says at the call site that
/// this allocates, which the in-place [`to_upper_in_place`] beside it does not.
to_upper_copy :: (s: string) -> string {
    if s.count == 0 {
        return make_string(null, 0);
    }
    buf := context.allocator(s.count);
    if buf == null {
        return make_string(null, 0);
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < s.count {
        c := (s.data + i).*;
        if c >= 97 {
            if c <= 122 {
                c = c - 32;
            }
        }
        (buf + i).* = c;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return make_string(buf, s.count);
}

/// A copy of `s` with ASCII uppercase letters lowered; other bytes unchanged.
///
/// The mirror of [`to_upper_copy`], and named the same way for the same reason. `'A'` is 65 and `'Z'` is
/// 90; adding 32 reaches `'a'`.
to_lower_copy :: (s: string) -> string {
    if s.count == 0 {
        return make_string(null, 0);
    }
    buf := context.allocator(s.count);
    if buf == null {
        return make_string(null, 0);
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < s.count {
        c := (s.data + i).*;
        if c >= 65 {
            if c <= 90 {
                c = c + 32;
            }
        }
        (buf + i).* = c;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return make_string(buf, s.count);
}

/// Releases a string this module allocated.
///
/// **The routine a caller must not forget** — there are no destructors, so nothing else frees it.
///
/// Safe on an empty string **that an allocating routine returned**: `substring`, `to_upper` and the rest return
/// `null` data for a zero count, and `allocator_free` of null is a no-op, so a caller need not special-case the
/// empty case.
///
/// **Not** safe on the `""` *literal*, and that distinction is sharper than it looks: a literal's `data` is a
/// real pointer into the program's own read-only data with a count of zero, so freeing it hands the allocator
/// something it never allocated. Natively that aborts; the comptime VM satisfies `malloc`/`free` from its own
/// region (ADR-0061) and quietly ignores a pointer it does not recognise, so the same program **runs under
/// `jr run` and dies as a binary** — a divergence only a native run can see. ADR-0157 §5 found it by writing
/// `out := "";` and freeing `out` a few lines later, which is a shape any accumulate-into-a-string loop has.
/// Start such a loop with `substring("", 0, 0)` instead.
///
/// More generally: must be given a string from one of the allocating routines above.
free_string :: (s: string) {
    if s.data != null {
        context.allocator_free(s.data);
    }
}

/// Adopts an allocation as a `string`, taking ownership of it.
///
/// `data` must point at `count` bytes from `context.allocator`, and after this call the string owns them:
/// `free_string` is what releases them and nothing else should. It is the mirror of `free_string`, and the
/// two are the whole ownership story for a computed string.
///
/// **This used to be module-private**, on the stated ground that "a caller has no business constructing a
/// `string` from raw parts" — and that was right while the only callers were the allocating routines below,
/// which all build a string from *other strings*. ADR-0156's JSON parser is the first caller that genuinely
/// cannot: it unescapes `\n` and `\uXXXX` into a buffer it sizes itself, so its result exists as bytes
/// before it exists as a string. Exporting a *named* transfer is better than the alternatives, which were
/// for JSON to assign the fields itself — the same five lines with no shared contract to read — or for
/// `String` to grow an `unescape` that belongs to JSON's grammar rather than to strings.
///
/// The name says the contract: `adopt` takes over an allocation, where a `make`/`from_bytes` would suggest
/// it copies. It does not copy, and a caller who wants a copy wants `substring`.
adopt :: (data: *u8, count: s64) -> string {
    return make_string(data, count);
}

/// Views `count` bytes at `data` as a `string`, **without** taking ownership.
///
/// The counterpart to [`adopt`], and the same construction with the opposite contract: the result must
/// **not** be given to `free_string`, because somebody else owns the bytes. It is what a routine returning a
/// *slice of its argument* needs — `File_Utilities.base_name` hands back part of the path it was given, and a
/// version that copied would make every caller free something they did not ask to own.
///
/// Two names over one operation, and that is the point: the construction is identical and the **obligation**
/// is not, so the call site says which one it is. A single `make` would leave every reader working out the
/// ownership from context, which is how a double free gets written. ADR-0157 §4 records the pair.
///
/// A borrowed string is valid exactly as long as the bytes behind it, which for a slice of an argument means
/// "as long as the argument". Nothing checks that — there is no lifetime system here — so the rule is the
/// call site's to keep, and naming it is the most the language can do today.
borrow :: (data: *u8, count: s64) -> string {
    return make_string(data, count);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Trimming (ADR-0197 §7)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The bytes `trim` removes by default: space, tab, newline, carriage return.
///
/// A constant rather than a literal at three call sites, so a reader grepping for what "whitespace" means
/// here finds one answer. Jai's equivalent is `DEFAULT_SPACES`; the name is spelled out because `SPACES`
/// suggests only `' '`.
DEFAULT_WHITESPACE :: " \t\n\r";

/// `s` with leading bytes from `chars` removed.
///
/// # Why this returns a view and allocates nothing
///
/// A trim's result is always a **contiguous run inside the input**, so it needs no copy — and a version
/// that allocated would make the commonest string operation in any program require an allocator and a
/// matching free. Jai's returns a view for the same reason.
///
/// The consequence, stated because it matters: the result **borrows** `s`, so it is valid exactly as long
/// as `s` is. A caller who needs to outlive it copies with [`substring`].
trim_left_chars :: (s: string, chars: string) -> string {
    start := 0;
    while start < s.count {
        if !contains_byte(chars, (s.data + start).*) {
            break;
        }
        start = start + 1;
    }
    return borrow(s.data + start, s.count - start);
}

/// `s` with trailing bytes from `chars` removed. Borrows `s`, like [`trim_left_chars`].
trim_right_chars :: (s: string, chars: string) -> string {
    stop := s.count;
    while stop > 0 {
        if !contains_byte(chars, (s.data + stop - 1).*) {
            break;
        }
        stop = stop - 1;
    }
    return borrow(s.data, stop);
}

/// `s` with leading and trailing bytes from `chars` removed. Borrows `s`.
trim_chars :: (s: string, chars: string) -> string {
    return trim_right_chars(trim_left_chars(s, chars), chars);
}

/// `s` with leading whitespace removed. Borrows `s`.
trim_left :: (s: string) -> string {
    return trim_left_chars(s, DEFAULT_WHITESPACE);
}

/// `s` with trailing whitespace removed. Borrows `s`.
trim_right :: (s: string) -> string {
    return trim_right_chars(s, DEFAULT_WHITESPACE);
}

/// `s` with leading and trailing whitespace removed. Borrows `s`.
///
/// The one this library was asked for, and it was missing: a build script reading `git rev-parse`'s output
/// had to strip the trailing newline with arithmetic, which is the shape a standard library exists to
/// remove.
///
/// **A separate name rather than a defaulted parameter.** Jai writes `trim(s, chars := DEFAULT_SPACES)`;
/// this library splits the two, because a defaulted parameter of a *variadic-free* procedure is exactly
/// what ADR-0139 §1 made unreachable once a variadic follows it — and more simply because two names read
/// better at a call site than one name whose behaviour depends on an argument the reader cannot see.
trim :: (s: string) -> string {
    return trim_chars(s, DEFAULT_WHITESPACE);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Searching
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether `s` contains the byte `b`.
///
/// Jai's second `contains` overload. A separate name here because this library has no overloading on
/// parameter type, and `contains_byte` says at the call site which question is being asked — which a
/// reader of `contains(s, x)` cannot tell without knowing `x`'s type.
contains_byte :: (s: string, b: u8) -> bool {
    return find_byte(s, b) >= 0;
}

/// The index of the first `b` in `s`, or `-1`.
find_byte :: (s: string, b: u8) -> s64 {
    i := 0;
    while i < s.count {
        if (s.data + i).* == b {
            return i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

/// The index of the last `b` in `s`, or `-1`.
find_byte_from_right :: (s: string, b: u8) -> s64 {
    i := s.count;
    while i > 0 {
        if (s.data + i - 1).* == b {
            return i - 1;
        }
        i = i - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

/// The index of the last occurrence of `needle` in `s`, or `-1`.
///
/// The mirror of [`find`], which searches from the left. Both are needed and neither implies the other:
/// splitting a path at its last separator is the ordinary use, and doing it with a left search means
/// looping.
find_from_right :: (s: string, needle: string) -> s64 {
    if needle.count == 0 {
        return s.count;
    }
    if needle.count > s.count {
        return -1;
    }
    start := s.count - needle.count;
    while start >= 0 {
        if region_equal(s, start, needle) {
            return start;
        }
        start = start - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

/// The index of the first byte of `s` that appears in `chars`, or `-1`.
find_any :: (s: string, chars: string) -> s64 {
    i := 0;
    while i < s.count {
        if contains_byte(chars, (s.data + i).*) {
            return i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

/// Whether `s` contains any byte from `chars`.
contains_any :: (s: string, chars: string) -> bool {
    return find_any(s, chars) >= 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Splitting and joining
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Splits `s` at the first `separator`.
///
/// Returns whether it was found, and the parts either side — the separator itself is in neither. When it
/// is absent, `left` is all of `s` and `right` is empty, which is what a caller parsing `key=value` wants
/// for a line with no `=`: the whole line is the key.
///
/// Both parts **borrow** `s`, so they live exactly as long as it does.
split_from_left :: (s: string, separator: string) -> (bool, string, string) {
    at := find(s, separator);
    if at < 0 {
        return false, s, borrow(null, 0);
    }
    rest := at + separator.count;
    return true, borrow(s.data, at), borrow(s.data + rest, s.count - rest);
}

/// Splits `s` at the **last** `separator`. Borrows `s`, like [`split_from_left`].
split_from_right :: (s: string, separator: string) -> (bool, string, string) {
    at := find_from_right(s, separator);
    if at < 0 {
        return false, s, borrow(null, 0);
    }
    rest := at + separator.count;
    return true, borrow(s.data, at), borrow(s.data + rest, s.count - rest);
}

/// How many pieces [`split`] would produce.
///
/// Exposed because `split` allocates, and a caller who only needs the count — validating a CSV's column
/// count, say — should not have to allocate to learn it.
split_count :: (s: string, separator: string) -> s64 {
    if separator.count == 0 {
        return 1;
    }
    n := 1;
    at := 0;
    while at <= s.count - separator.count {
        if region_equal(s, at, separator) {
            n = n + 1;
            at = at + separator.count;
        } else {
            at = at + 1;
        }
    }
    return n;
}

/// Every piece of `s` between occurrences of `separator`.
///
/// Allocates the **view** through `context.allocator`; the pieces themselves borrow `s` and are not
/// copied, so freeing the view is enough and the pieces die with `s`. An empty separator yields one piece,
/// the whole string — the alternative, a piece per byte, is never what a caller means.
///
/// Adjacent separators produce empty pieces, and a leading or trailing one produces an empty piece at that
/// end. That is what makes `split` and [`join`] inverses, which is the property a round trip needs.
split :: (s: string, separator: string) -> []string {
    n := split_count(s, separator);
    raw := context.allocator(n * size_of(string));
    if raw == null {
        empty: []string;
        return empty;
    }
    out := typed(string, raw);
    if separator.count == 0 {
        (out + 0).* = s;
        return view(out, 1);
    }
    written := 0;
    start := 0;
    at := 0;
    while at <= s.count - separator.count {
        if region_equal(s, at, separator) {
            (out + written).* = borrow(s.data + start, at - start);
            written = written + 1;
            at = at + separator.count;
            start = at;
        } else {
            at = at + 1;
        }
    }
    (out + written).* = borrow(s.data + start, s.count - start);
    return view(out, n);
}

/// Every piece of `s` between occurrences of the byte `b`.
split_by_byte :: (s: string, b: u8) -> []string {
    one: [1]u8;
    one[0] = b;
    return split(s, borrow(*one[0], 1));
}

/// `pieces` concatenated with `separator` between them.
///
/// Allocates the result through `context.allocator`. The inverse of [`split`] for the same separator,
/// which is why both treat empty pieces as real.
join :: (pieces: []string, separator: string) -> string {
    if pieces.count == 0 {
        return borrow(null, 0);
    }
    total := separator.count * (pieces.count - 1);
    i := 0;
    while i < pieces.count {
        total = total + pieces[i].count;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if total == 0 {
        return borrow(null, 0);
    }
    buf := context.allocator(total);
    if buf == null {
        return borrow(null, 0);
    }
    at := 0;
    i = 0;
    while i < pieces.count {
        if i > 0 {
            copy_bytes(buf + at, separator.data, 0, separator.count);
            at = at + separator.count;
        }
        copy_bytes(buf + at, pieces[i].data, 0, pieces[i].count);
        at = at + pieces[i].count;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return adopt(buf, total);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Replacing
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// `s` with every occurrence of `old` replaced by `new`, and how many were replaced.
///
/// Allocates through `context.allocator`. An empty `old` replaces nothing and returns a copy, rather than
/// inserting `new` between every byte — which no caller means and which would make the count unbounded.
replace :: (s: string, old: string, new: string) -> (string, s64) {
    if old.count == 0 {
        return substring(s, 0, s.count), 0;
    }
    occurrences := split_count(s, old) - 1;
    if occurrences == 0 {
        return substring(s, 0, s.count), 0;
    }
    total := s.count + occurrences * (new.count - old.count);
    if total == 0 {
        return borrow(null, 0), occurrences;
    }
    buf := context.allocator(total);
    if buf == null {
        return borrow(null, 0), 0;
    }
    out := 0;
    at := 0;
    while at < s.count {
        if at <= s.count - old.count && region_equal(s, at, old) {
            copy_bytes(buf + out, new.data, 0, new.count);
            out = out + new.count;
            at = at + old.count;
        } else {
            (buf + out).* = (s.data + at).*;
            out = out + 1;
            at = at + 1;
        }
    }
    return adopt(buf, total), occurrences;
}

/// Overwrites, **in place**, every byte of `s` that appears in `chars` with `replacement`.
///
/// Jai's `replace_chars`, and like Jai's it mutates: a `string` is a `{data, count}` pair, so writing
/// through `s.data` changes whatever the caller is holding. Returns nothing, so a call site cannot mistake
/// it for the allocating [`replace`].
///
/// **A string literal must not be passed.** A literal's bytes are in the program's read-only data, and
/// writing to them is a fault — the same rule that makes freeing a literal wrong (recorded by ADR-0157).
replace_chars_in_place :: (s: string, chars: string, replacement: u8) {
    i := 0;
    while i < s.count {
        if contains_byte(chars, (s.data + i).*) {
            (s.data + i).* = replacement;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

/// Raises ASCII lowercase letters in `s` to uppercase, **in place**.
///
/// The in-place pair of [`to_upper`], which copies. Both exist because both uses are real: a parser
/// normalising a buffer it owns wants no allocation, and a caller keeping the original wants the copy.
/// Jai has the same pair, named `to_upper_in_place` and `to_upper_copy`.
to_upper_in_place :: (s: string) {
    i := 0;
    while i < s.count {
        (s.data + i).* = ascii_upper((s.data + i).*);
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

/// Lowers ASCII uppercase letters in `s`, **in place**.
to_lower_in_place :: (s: string) {
    i := 0;
    while i < s.count {
        (s.data + i).* = ascii_lower((s.data + i).*);
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Case-insensitive comparison
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether `a` and `b` are equal ignoring ASCII case.
equal_nocase :: (a: string, b: string) -> bool {
    if a.count != b.count {
        return false;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < a.count {
        if ascii_lower((a.data + i).*) != ascii_lower((b.data + i).*) {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Like [`compare`], ignoring ASCII case.
compare_nocase :: (a: string, b: string) -> s64 {
    n := a.count;
    if b.count < n {
        n = b.count;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < n {
        x := ascii_lower((a.data + i).*);
        y := ascii_lower((b.data + i).*);
        if x != y {
            if x < y {
                return -1;
            }
            return 1;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if a.count == b.count {
        return 0;
    }
    if a.count < b.count {
        return -1;
    }
    return 1;
}

/// Whether `s` starts with `prefix`, ignoring ASCII case.
starts_with_nocase :: (s: string, prefix: string) -> bool {
    if prefix.count > s.count {
        return false;
    }
    return equal_nocase(borrow(s.data, prefix.count), prefix);
}

/// Whether `s` ends with `suffix`, ignoring ASCII case.
ends_with_nocase :: (s: string, suffix: string) -> bool {
    if suffix.count > s.count {
        return false;
    }
    start := s.count - suffix.count;
    return equal_nocase(borrow(s.data + start, suffix.count), suffix);
}

/// The index of the first occurrence of `needle` in `haystack` ignoring ASCII case, or `-1`.
///
/// The case-insensitive twin of [`find`]. It exists because the family was **asymmetric**: `equal_nocase`,
/// `compare_nocase`, `starts_with_nocase` and `ends_with_nocase` all had a case-folding version and the two
/// *search* routines did not, so a caller matching a filename case-insensitively had to lower a copy of the
/// whole haystack first — an allocation, for a comparison.
find_nocase :: (haystack: string, needle: string) -> s64 {
    if needle.count > haystack.count {
        return -1;
    }
    last := haystack.count - needle.count;
    start := 0;
    while start <= last {
        if region_equal_nocase(haystack, start, needle) {
            return start;
        }
        start = start + 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

/// Whether `needle` appears anywhere in `haystack`, ignoring ASCII case.
///
/// Jai's `contains_nocase`. Delegates rather than looping, so the two cannot disagree about a match — the
/// same reason [`contains`] delegates to [`find`].
contains_nocase :: (haystack: string, needle: string) -> bool {
    return find_nocase(haystack, needle) >= 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Parsing
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Reads a decimal integer from the front of `s`.
///
/// Returns the value, whether anything was read, and the rest of `s`. The three-value shape is Jai's
/// `to_integer`, and the *remainder* is what makes it usable in a loop — a version returning only the value
/// forces the caller to re-find where the number ended.
///
/// An optional leading `-` or `+` is accepted. Overflow is **not** reported: the accumulation traps, which
/// is this language's answer to arithmetic that does not fit (ADR-0002) and is a better one than a silently
/// wrapped value.
to_integer :: (s: string) -> (s64, bool, string) {
    at := 0;
    negative := false;
    if at < s.count {
        c := (s.data + at).*;
        if c == 45 {
            negative = true;
            at = at + 1;
        }
        if c == 43 {
            at = at + 1;
        }
    }
    digits := 0;
    value := 0;
    while at < s.count {
        c := (s.data + at).*;
        if c < 48 || c > 57 {
            break;
        }
        value = value * 10 + cast(s64, c - 48);
        digits = digits + 1;
        at = at + 1;
    }
    if digits == 0 {
        return 0, false, s;
    }
    if negative {
        value = -value;
    }
    return value, true, borrow(s.data + at, s.count - at);
}

/// Whether `s` is exactly `"true"` or `"false"`, and which.
///
/// Returns the value and whether it parsed. Case-sensitive, because a configuration format that accepts
/// `True` and `TRUE` has to say so, and this is the primitive such a format is built from.
to_bool :: (s: string) -> (bool, bool) {
    if equal(s, "true") {
        return true, true;
    }
    if equal(s, "false") {
        return false, true;
    }
    return false, false;
}

/// Reads a decimal floating-point number from the front of `s` (ADR-0198 §1).
///
/// Returns the value, whether anything was read, and the rest of `s` — the same three-value shape as
/// [`to_integer`], for the same reason: a caller in a loop needs to know where the number ended.
///
/// # Why libc does the conversion
///
/// The **extent** is found here and the **value** by `strtod`, which is the split ADR-0156 chose for
/// `modules/JSON` and it is right for the same reason. Converting a decimal to the nearest `float64`
/// *exactly* is a genuinely hard problem — a wrong last bit is a wrong **answer** rather than a wrong
/// approximation, which is why `Math` ships no `sqrt` (ADR-0112) — so the platform's correctly-rounded
/// conversion does it. Accumulating `digit + value * 10.0` in Jairs would be twenty lines and wrong in the
/// last place for inputs a build script really has, and wrong in a way no test written by the same author
/// would catch.
///
/// The extent cannot be left to `strtod` either: it accepts `0x1p3`, `inf`, `nan` and a leading `+` on
/// platforms this does not want to vary by, so the grammar accepted here is spelled out — an optional sign,
/// digits, an optional `.` and digits, an optional `e`/`E` with an optional sign and digits — and `strtod`
/// only ever sees bytes that already matched it.
///
/// # It needs an installed allocator
///
/// `strtod` reads to a **NUL**, and a Jairs `string` has none (ADR-0004) — the trap that made
/// `glShaderSource` compile whatever followed a shader in memory. So the matched bytes are copied into a
/// NUL-terminated scratch buffer from `context.allocator`, and freed before returning. That is the same
/// obligation the rest of this module's allocating half already has, and forgetting is not silent:
/// `context.allocator` is null until set and a call through it traps.
///
/// `talloc` would be the tidier scratch and is deliberately not used: it lives in `modules/Basic`, and this
/// module imports nothing (see the module docs). One parse is not worth that dependency.
string_to_float :: (s: string) -> (float64, bool, string) {
    n := float_extent(s);
    if n == 0 {
        return 0.0, false, s;
    }
    scratch := context.allocator(n + 1);
    if scratch == null {
        return 0.0, false, s;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < n {
        (scratch + i).* = (s.data + i).*;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    (scratch + n).* = cast(u8, 0);
    ignored: *u8;
    value := strtod(scratch, *ignored);
    context.allocator_free(scratch);
    return value, true, borrow(s.data + n, s.count - n);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// C strings
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The length of a NUL-terminated C string, not counting the NUL.
///
/// Jai's own name. Returns 0 for a null pointer rather than trapping, because the usual caller is reading a
/// result out of a `#foreign` call that is *documented* to return null on failure — `getenv`, `strerror`,
/// `SDL_GetError` — and making the failure path a trap would force a null check at every one of them.
c_style_strlen :: (str: *u8) -> s64 {
    if str == null {
        return 0;
    }
    n := 0;
    while (str + n).* != cast(u8, 0) {
        n = n + 1;
    }
    return n;
}

/// A `string` viewing a NUL-terminated C string, **without** copying it.
///
/// Jai's own name, and the routine this language needed most for FFI: every `#foreign` call that returns
/// `*u8` returns a C string, and a Jairs `string` is `{data, count}` with no NUL (ADR-0004), so the two
/// representations meet exactly here. Before this existed each caller counted the bytes itself.
///
/// **Borrows.** The result's lifetime is the C string's, so a pointer into a libc static buffer — `strerror`,
/// `getenv` — must be copied with [`copy_string`] before the next call overwrites it. Named `to_string` after
/// Jai rather than `borrow_c_string`, because the name a reader will search for is Jai's; the borrowing is
/// what the doc is for, and [`borrow`] beside it makes the pair legible.
to_string :: (str: *u8) -> string {
    return borrow(str, c_style_strlen(str));
}

/// A NUL-terminated copy of `s`, for handing to C.
///
/// Jai's own name, and the exact inverse of [`to_string`]. The pair is the whole FFI boundary: C strings are
/// terminated and Jairs strings are counted (ADR-0004), so one direction can borrow and the other cannot.
///
/// # This is not optional, and the cost of skipping it is silence
///
/// **A string literal's bytes are not followed by a NUL.** Passing `"PATH".data` to `getenv` reads whatever
/// the linker happened to place next, looks up a name nobody set, and returns null — no diagnostic, no trap,
/// just a wrong answer. That is the same defect that made `glShaderSource` compile the bytes after a shader
/// (recorded in AGENTS.md), and `tests/corpus/valid/148-string-ffi-glob.jr` was written against `getenv`
/// and hit it immediately, which is how this procedure came to exist.
///
/// The failure looks like a *divergence* at first and is not: both engines agree, because both are reading
/// past the same string. That is worth knowing, because it means the differential harness cannot catch it.
///
/// **Allocates**, from `context.allocator`, and the caller frees with [`free_string`] — the same obligation
/// as [`concat`] and [`substring`], because a terminated copy is a copy. Borrowing is impossible here by
/// construction: there is nowhere to put the NUL that is not someone else's byte.
///
/// Returns null if the allocation fails, which is what C already expects from a failed `strdup`.
to_c_string :: (s: string) -> *u8 {
    buf := context.allocator(s.count + 1);
    if buf == null {
        return null;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < s.count {
        (buf + i).* = (s.data + i).*;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    (buf + s.count).* = cast(u8, 0);
    return buf;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Glob matching
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether `s` matches `pattern`, where `*` is any run of bytes and `?` is exactly one.
///
/// Jai's own name and semantics, and confirmed in real Jai code rather than assumed: `polgartom/astex` and
/// `okmatija/Prizm` both call it, and both call it on paths. A build script wants it for the same thing.
///
/// # Iterative with one backtrack point, not recursive
///
/// The textbook recursion is four lines and takes exponential time on `"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"` against
/// `"*a*a*a*a*a"` — a pattern a caller writes by accident. This keeps the position of the last `*` and the
/// input position it was tried at, so a mismatch resumes one byte later instead of unwinding: linear in the
/// input times the number of `*`s, and it cannot overflow a stack.
///
/// An empty pattern matches only an empty `s`, and `"*"` matches everything including `""`. There is no
/// escape for a literal `*` or `?`, matching Jai — a caller needing one wants a real regex.
wildcard_match :: (s: string, pattern: string) -> bool {
    si := 0;
    pi := 0;
    // Where to resume if the current attempt fails: the `*` to stretch, and how far it had stretched.
    star := -1;
    star_si := 0;
    while si < s.count {
        if pi < pattern.count && byte_at(pattern, pi) == QUESTION {
            si = si + 1;
            pi = pi + 1;
        } else if pi < pattern.count && byte_at(pattern, pi) == STAR {
            star = pi;
            pi = pi + 1;
            star_si = si;
        } else if pi < pattern.count && byte_at(pattern, pi) == byte_at(s, si) {
            si = si + 1;
            pi = pi + 1;
        } else if star >= 0 {
            // Stretch the remembered `*` by one byte and try the rest of the pattern again.
            pi = star + 1;
            star_si = star_si + 1;
            si = star_si;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }
    // Trailing `*`s may still match the empty remainder; anything else cannot.
    while pi < pattern.count && byte_at(pattern, pi) == STAR {
        pi = pi + 1;
    }
    return pi == pattern.count;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Paths
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Deliberately **absent**: the path operations Jai keeps in `modules/String` — `path_filename`,
// `path_extension`, `path_join` and the rest — because `modules/File_Utilities` already has them
// (`base_name`, `extension`, `join`, `directory_name`, `stem`, `is_absolute`, `normalise`). Adding a second
// set here would give a reader two answers to one question, which is worse than either.
//
// That is a placement difference from Jai rather than a gap, and it is the *better* placement: a path is a
// filesystem concept, and `File_Utilities` is where the filesystem lives.

#scope_module

/// The bytes `wildcard_match` treats as its two metacharacters.
STAR :: 42;
QUESTION :: 63;

/// libc `strtod(3)`, for the exact decimal-to-`float64` conversion (ADR-0198 §1).
///
/// Reachable because ADR-0114 let a float cross the `#foreign` boundary. The `end` out-parameter is ignored:
/// [`string_to_float`] has already found the number's extent with its own scanner, which has to happen
/// anyway because `strtod`'s grammar is wider than the one this accepts.
///
/// **This is the module's only foreign declaration**, and the module docs' claim that it "imports nothing at
/// all" is about `#import` and stays true — libc is linked by every program this compiler produces.
strtod :: (text: *u8, end: **u8) -> float64 #foreign libc "strtod";

libc :: #system_library "c";

/// Whether `s`'s bytes at `at` match `needle`, ignoring ASCII case.
///
/// The case-folding twin of [`region_equal`], and separate from it rather than a flag on it: a flag makes
/// every caller of the exact comparison pass a `false` that means nothing to it.
region_equal_nocase :: (s: string, at: s64, needle: string) -> bool {
    if at < 0 || at + needle.count > s.count {
        return false;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < needle.count {
        if ascii_lower((s.data + at + i).*) != ascii_lower((needle.data + i).*) {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// How many bytes at the front of `s` form a decimal floating-point number, or 0 if none do.
///
/// The grammar is spelled out here rather than delegated to `strtod`, which is the whole point of the split
/// (see [`string_to_float`]): an optional sign, digits, an optional `.` with digits, an optional exponent.
/// At least one digit is required **before** the exponent, so `"e5"` and `"."` read nothing.
float_extent :: (s: string) -> s64 {
    i := 0;
    if i < s.count && (byte_at(s, i) == PLUS || byte_at(s, i) == MINUS) {
        i = i + 1;
    }
    digits := 0;
    while i < s.count && is_ascii_digit(byte_at(s, i)) {
        i = i + 1;
        digits = digits + 1;
    }
    if i < s.count && byte_at(s, i) == DOT {
        i = i + 1;
        while i < s.count && is_ascii_digit(byte_at(s, i)) {
            i = i + 1;
            digits = digits + 1;
        }
    }
    if digits == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    // An exponent is only part of the number if it is **complete**: `"1e"` is the number 1 followed by an
    // `e`, not a malformed number, so the position is only committed once a digit is seen.
    if i < s.count && (byte_at(s, i) == LOWER_E || byte_at(s, i) == UPPER_E) {
        after := i + 1;
        if after < s.count && (byte_at(s, after) == PLUS || byte_at(s, after) == MINUS) {
            after = after + 1;
        }
        exponent_digits := 0;
        while after < s.count && is_ascii_digit(byte_at(s, after)) {
            after = after + 1;
            exponent_digits = exponent_digits + 1;
        }
        if exponent_digits > 0 {
            i = after;
        }
    }
    return i;
}

/// Whether `c` is an ASCII digit, taking the `s64` [`byte_at`] returns so a `-1` at the end of the string
/// answers `false` rather than needing a length check at every call.
is_ascii_digit :: (c: s64) -> bool {
    return c >= ZERO && c <= NINE;
}

PLUS :: 43;
MINUS :: 45;
DOT :: 46;
ZERO :: 48;
NINE :: 57;
UPPER_E :: 69;
LOWER_E :: 101;

/// Whether `s`'s bytes at `at` match `needle`.
///
/// The one comparison every search here shares, so `find`, `find_from_right`, `split` and `replace` cannot
/// disagree about what a match is — which they would if each wrote its own loop.
region_equal :: (s: string, at: s64, needle: string) -> bool {
    if at < 0 || at + needle.count > s.count {
        return false;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < needle.count {
        if (s.data + at + i).* != (needle.data + i).* {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// `c` raised to ASCII uppercase.
///
/// Duplicated from `Basic.to_upper` rather than imported, because this module deliberately does not depend
/// on `Basic` — a string library that pulls in `print` and an allocator's declarations makes every importer
/// pay for them. Two lines of arithmetic is a cheaper price than that dependency, and the pair is checked
/// against each other by `basic_and_string_agree_about_ascii_case`.
ascii_upper :: (c: u8) -> u8 {
    if c >= 97 && c <= 122 {
        return c - 32;
    }
    return c;
}

/// `c` lowered to ASCII lowercase. The mirror of [`ascii_upper`].
ascii_lower :: (c: u8) -> u8 {
    if c >= 65 && c <= 90 {
        return c + 32;
    }
    return c;
}

/// Builds a `string` from a data pointer and a count.
///
/// Module-private: a caller has no business constructing a `string` from raw parts, and the allocating routines
/// are the supported way to get one. It exists because the fields are assignable and this is the one place that
/// should assign them.
make_string :: (data: *u8, count: s64) -> string {
    r: string;
    r.data = data;
    r.count = count;
    return r;
}

/// Copies `count` bytes from `src` into `dest` at byte offset `at`.
///
/// The one loop the allocating routines share, named so a bug in it is fixed once. `dest` is `*u8` and `at` is a
/// byte offset, so `dest + at` is the write cursor — pointer arithmetic on `*u8` is element-scaled by 1
/// (ADR-0064), which is what makes a byte offset and a pointer offset the same number here.
copy_bytes :: (dest: *u8, src: *u8, at: s64, count: s64) {
    i := 0;
    while i < count {
        (dest + at + i).* = (src + i).*;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}
